{"c":[{"a":"chatgpt-codex-connector","b":"**\u003csub\u003e\u003csub\u003e![P1 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P1-orange?style=flat)\u003c/sub\u003e\u003c/sub\u003e  Require given and family names to identify the same person**  When an obituary mentions several people, these independent token searches can combine one person's surname with another person's given name and assign the deceased's dates to that fabricated match. For example, resolving `Charles Hawks (1932-2017). Survived by Gaynor Jones.` against Gaynor Claire Hawks, born 1932 and died 2017, returns a high-confidence candidate backed by Charles's obituary. Require the matching name tokens to form a single person's name before using nearby dates; otherwise the confirmation screen presents unrelated records a","dbid":4397474103,"l":190,"p":"src/lib/server/identity.ts"}],"id":"PRRT_kwDOVZZb886xANuf"}
{"c":[{"a":"chatgpt-codex-connector","b":"**\u003csub\u003e\u003csub\u003e![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)\u003c/sub\u003e\u003c/sub\u003e  Preserve source dates when the stone lacks those dates**  When a stone has no birth or death date, `compareYear` returns `none` even if the source explicitly states that year, so these assignments discard the source's known dates. Reproducing with two records for Gaynor Hawks, `(1932-2017)` and `(1942-2020)`, produces separate candidates whose birth and death fields are both null. The confirmation UI consequently displays identical names and `unknown – unknown` for people the matcher already distinguished by dates. Preserve stated source years independently of whether the stone can confirm them, while kee","dbid":4397474106,"l":203,"p":"src/lib/server/identity.ts"}],"id":"PRRT_kwDOVZZb886xANug"}
{"c":[{"a":"chatgpt-codex-connector","b":"**\u003csub\u003e\u003csub\u003e![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)\u003c/sub\u003e\u003c/sub\u003e  Recognize supported facts composed entirely of short words**  If the only verified biographical fact uses words shorter than five characters, `topicStems` returns an empty set and this condition always excludes it, even when the paragraph repeats it exactly. For example, the verified quote `was a chef` passes `hasEvidence`, but grounding `I was a chef.` returns `insufficient`. This sends visitors to an epitaph claiming the records contain nothing beyond the stone despite a usable occupational fact. Add a matching fallback for facts without long topic words rather than treating them as unused unconditional","dbid":4397474112,"l":218,"p":"src/lib/server/narrative.ts"}],"id":"PRRT_kwDOVZZb886xANuj"}
{"c":[{"a":"chatgpt-codex-connector","b":"**\u003csub\u003e\u003csub\u003e![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)\u003c/sub\u003e\u003c/sub\u003e  Preserve record-identifying query parameters during deduplication**  When search returns different records distinguished by query parameters, this key collapses them into one result before identity matching. For example, `/obituaries/print?o_id=5589051` and `/obituaries/print?o_id=5589052` produce identical keys; this URL format already appears in `benchmarks/results/search-1.json`. Since `SerpApiProvider.search` keeps only the first occurrence, an unrelated obituary can discard the actual matching record, causing research to return no candidate despite finding a usable source. Preserve content-identifyin","dbid":4401395773,"l":14,"p":"src/lib/server/search/url.ts"}],"id":"PRRT_kwDOVZZb886xJi-9"}
{"c":[{"a":"chatgpt-codex-connector","b":"**\u003csub\u003e\u003csub\u003e![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)\u003c/sub\u003e\u003c/sub\u003e  Parse day-first and numeric dates before checking year conflicts**  When a source uses a day-first or numeric date, this expression misses its explicitly stated year and treats it as absent rather than conflicting. A direct reproduction against the stone born in 1932 and died in 2017 accepts `Gaynor Hawks, born 10 March 1940, died 2017.` as a medium-confidence candidate; `born 03/10/1940` behaves identically, while `born March 10, 1940` correctly excludes the record. This allows a namesake with contradictory dates into the confirmation and story flow. Recognize these common date formats before applying th","dbid":4403126240,"l":124,"p":"src/lib/server/identity.ts"}],"id":"PRRT_kwDOVZZb886xNpUZ"}
{"c":[{"a":"chatgpt-codex-connector","b":"**\u003csub\u003e\u003csub\u003e![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)\u003c/sub\u003e\u003c/sub\u003e  Use a directional birth-year interval for age-derived dates**  When a record supplies an age at death, the symmetric `bornSlack = 1` also accepts a birth year one year later than `died - age`, which is impossible. A reproduction with a stone born in 1933 and died in 2017 and the record `Gaynor Hawks, age 85, died December 27, 2017.` returns a high-confidence candidate, although someone aged 85 in 2017 must have been born in 1931 or 1932. Represent the possible birth years as the directional interval `[died - age - 1, died - age]` and use that interval for conflict checks and grouping so incompatible names","dbid":4403308434,"l":162,"p":"src/lib/server/identity.ts"}],"id":"PRRT_kwDOVZZb886xOEhI"}
